A||||2||Firebug||2.8L camshaft choice||||1||
Z||000000||Firebug||03-22-2001||10:44 PM||||I looked through the archive and found out that going up to the 3.1 would be a waste of time after the loss of compression.  I decided to rebuild my 2.8 with Northern Auto parts from advice from this column.  Now I am wondering what to do for a cam.  I have a stock rebuild kit with Moly rings and a high volume oil pump.  The Fiero has an automatic, so a high RPM cam would be out.  I want something more fun than stock, but not overly wild for a basically stock engine.  I don't plan on any wild mods, but would like a little more power.  What do you guys recommend or don't like?<P>Thanks,<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierosound||03-23-2001||12:35 AM||||This website has "Mild to Wild" mods you may want to consider in your rebuild.  Some mods aren't outrageously expensive.  He also lists a selection of cams and their specs.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.users.qwest.net/~racing2win/mild2wld.htm#2800V6cams" TARGET=_blank>http://www.users.qwest.net/~racing2win/mild2wld.htm#2800V6cams</A> <P>||||reg||10
Z||000002||Chris McDonald||03-23-2001||07:07 AM||||Scott,<P>Check into Crane Cams.  Back in 94 I purchased a compu cam (I think it has a new name now) and ran a set of 1.6 ratio rocker arms.  <P>I was also running a 3.1.  If you order the correct pistons and have the machine work done right you should not have any compression drop.  If you use the stock 3.1 pistons for the 3.1 aluminum head motors you will lose compression due to the higher CC volume of the 2.8 heads.<P>The Camaro/Firebird 3.1 rear wheel drive engines had the correct compression piston dome as the cast heads are very simular. <P>The crane cam was very mild and gave a very strong mid-range pull and there was much more torque over the 2.8.  Make sure you pick up a torque converter to the specs of a mid/late 80s sunbird with the 1.8 overhead cam engine.  It has  MUCH higher stall and will allow very impressive launching speed.<P>Have fun.||||reg||1
